.\" Manual page for checkneko
.TH CHECKNEKO 1 "@MAN_DATE@" "Neko @PACKAGE_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
checkneko \- check Neko case files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B checkneko
.RB [ \-h ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR dir ]
.I file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B checkneko
checks a Neko case file without running Neko, and finds mistakes that would
otherwise only show once a simulation has started and read the mesh. It is
part of the Neko contrib tools and is installed only when Neko has been
configured with
.BR --enable-contrib .
It does not use MPI or the Neko library.
.PP
The case file is parsed with json-fortran, with the same settings as Neko,
so comments starting with
.BR / ,
.B !
or
.B #
and trailing commas are accepted exactly as Neko accepts them. It is then
checked for the following:
.IP \(bu 2
It must be valid against the JSON schema of the case file, see
.BR FILES .
Unknown keys in objects that only allow known ones, missing
keys, values of the wrong type and unknown values such as a misspelled
boundary condition type are errors, with a suggestion for a misspelled name.
As in Neko, a value read as a real must be written with a decimal point, and
one read as an integer without.
.IP \(bu 2
A value that names a constant must name one in
.BR case.constants ,
of the kind Neko reads there: an integer, a real or an array of the right
length.
.IP \(bu 2
The files the case reads must exist: the mesh, a restart file, field files of
initial and boundary conditions, probe points and similar. As for Neko, file
names that are not absolute are relative to the directory it runs in, which
is the current directory unless
.B \-d
is given.
.IP \(bu 2
For an
.B .nmsh
mesh, the labeled zones of the mesh are read and the
.B zone_indices
of the boundary conditions of the fluid and the scalars are checked against
them, as Neko does: every zone index must be in the mesh, no zone may have
two boundary conditions, and every zone of the mesh must have one. The
compressible solver only requires the zones to be in the mesh. The elements
are not read, so this is quick also for large meshes.
.IP \(bu 2
Two scalars must not have the same name.
.IP \(bu 2
Point zones that values refer to must be defined in
.BR case.point_zones .
.PP
Keys that appear twice in an object, of which Neko uses the first, and keys
that are not known but close to a known one, e.g.
.B output_checkpoint
for
.BR output_checkpoints ,
give warnings.
.PP
A table lists the labeled zones with their number of facets and the types of
the boundary conditions of the fluid and of each scalar on them, with the
.B name
of an entry in parentheses and
.B \-
where a field has none. A zone that a boundary condition refers to but the
mesh does not have is listed with
.B \-
facets, and the facets are
.BR ? " if the mesh was not read."
The scalars are named as in Neko:
.B s
for a single one and
.BR s_1 ", " s_2 ", ... for several, unless they have a"
.BR name .
.PP
Each problem is then printed as
.IB file : line :
.BR error :
or
.BR warning :
followed by the location in the case file, e.g.
.BR case.fluid.boundary_conditions[2].zone_indices ,
and a description. Entries of arrays are numbered from 1.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-d " dir"
The directory Neko will run in. File names in the case file that are not
absolute are relative to it. The default is the current directory.
.TP
.BI \-s " dir"
Read the schemas from
.IR dir ,
e.g.
.B doc/schemas
of the Neko source, instead of the installed ones, see
.BR FILES .
.TP
.B \-h
Print a summary of the options.
.PP
The options must come before the files. The value of an option can follow it
directly, as in
.BR \-d/scratch/run1 ,
and
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 if no errors were found, warnings do not count, and 1 if there were errors
in any of the case files or the command line was invalid.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B checkneko cylinder.case
Check a case that will run in the current directory.
.TP
.B checkneko \-d /scratch/run1 cases/cylinder.case
Check a case that will run in another directory.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I prefix/share/neko/schemas
The JSON schemas of the case file, from
.B doc/schemas
of the Neko source, read when
.B checkneko
starts and installed with it.
.B checkneko
finds them next to the
.B bin
directory it is installed in, also when it is run through a symbolic link.
When it is run from the build directory of a Neko source tree, it uses
.B doc/schemas
of that tree instead. The directory in use is printed at start.
.SH NOTES
.B checkneko
is built with the plain Fortran compiler
.B FC
and json-fortran. It can also be compiled on its own, for instance when Neko
is cross-compiled for another architecture than the machine
.B checkneko
is to run on, see
.B README.md
in
.BR contrib/checkneko .
Such a copy is given the schemas with
.BR \-s .
.PP
The checks cannot find everything that stops a run, for instance a user file
that does not define a user boundary condition, or settings that are valid
on their own but not supported together by a backend.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR neko (1),
.BR makeneko (1)
